# API Reference {#mainpage}

## Introduction
KEO provides a collection of libraries, headers and example applications to ease development of SPINE applications.
@n A typical SPINE application consists at least of
- a transport connection unit
- a transport connection configuration unit
- a SPINE unit

The transport connection unit provides access to physical network connections for general data exchange. 
For SPINE scenarios, this typically applies to SHIP (Smart Home IP, defined by EEBus Initiative e.V.). 
SHIP provides an encrypted peer to peer connection of two 'SHIP nodes' requiring mutual trust. 
The SHIP specification defines registration and verification procedures to gain certain trust levels and re-connection scenarios.
It also defines mechanisms to discover other SHIP nodes.

The transport connection configuration unit is typically project specific.
It implements rules to establish, accept or modify physical connections to remote communication partners.
E.g. for SHIP, this unit implements the desired verification procedure.


The SPINE unit represents a physical (or logical) appliance. 
It implements the abstract SPINE data model (defined by EEBus Initiative e.V.) and the SPINE protocol specifics (e.g. SPINE address schema).
The representation is hierarchically structured into 'devices', 'entities' and 'features' composing a SPINE device model.
To achieve interoperability a SPINE device model shall be designed according to official use cases provided by the EEBus Initiative e.V..
Vendor specific functionality is typically implemented within features.

The SPINE addressing schema is independent from any transport connection addressing schema.
For implementation reasons it is beneficial to abstract the transport connection addressing schema on SPINE device model level.
This allows sending SPINE datagrams from one feature to another one by using exclusively the SPINE address schema.

The SPINE data model defines a modular approach to combine diverse SPINE data model elements. 
Additionally the SPINE protocol specification defines mandatory, recommended and optional issues. 
This results in numerous possible combinations and opportunities for future extensions.
However, this flexibility is achieved at the expense of an easy and quick understanding of SPINE fundamentals, especially for beginners.
Hence, implementations of [example SPINE applications](@ref examples_framework) are provided to accelerate the process of understanding SPINE fundamentals and how to use the KEO SPINE API.

To create and implement SPINE applications the KEO SPINE API provides
- SHIP discovery (see [Discovery](@ref ship_discovery))
- SHIP verification (see [Security Handler](@ref ship_security_handler))
- SHIP registration and re-connection (see [Stack](@ref ship_stack))
- SPINE data model (see [SpineDatagram](@ref keo_datagram))
- SPINE device model (see [KEO SPINE](@ref framework))
- SPINE and API version information (see keo_spineVersion.h)
- SPINE Address schema abstraction (see keo_spine::SpineLink)
- SPINE Utility and convenience classes (see [Util](@ref keo_util))

@image html SPINE_Link_Application.svg

SPINE SubDevices are the sub devices of a gateway in terms of the SPINE specification.

## Threading
Callbacks are made from an arbitrary thread context. Applications should not rely on any assumptions here.
The framework intentionally implements no mechanism to make callbacks from one thread context (e.g. an event queue),
it is up to the application to take care of this.

## Logging
Logging can be configured using a simple ini file format.

### Log level
The following log level are defined and can be used during configuration.
  * %FATAL
  * %ERROR
  * %WARNING
  * %INFO
  * %TRACE
  * %DEBUG
  * %VERB_0
  * %VERB_1
  * %VERB_2
  * %VERB_3
  * %VERB_4
  * %VERB_5
  * %VERB_6
  * %VERB_7
  * %VERB_8
  * %VERB_9

### Logger configuration file
The logger configuration file is seperated in a global section and different logger sections.
Each section starts with the logger name enclosed in brackets (e.g. [keo_spine]).

#### Global section
- to_console=[true|false]<br>
Defines whether logging will be printed to stdout or not.

- to_file=[true|false]<br>
Defines whether logging will be written to file or not.

- filename=<name><br>
Defines the name of the file for logger output.

- level=<level><br>
The global log level. All log messages with a level higher or equal to this level will be printed out.<br>
E.g. level=WARNING means that all log messages with FATAL, ERROR and WARNING will be printed out.

#### Logger section
Logger sections start with the logger name enclosed in brackets (e.g. [keo_spine]).
The following configurations are possible.

- level=<level><br>
Overrides the global level configuration.<br>
All log messages with a level higher or equal to this level will be printed out.<br>
E.g. level=WARNING means that all log messages with FATAL, ERROR and WARNING will be printed out.

- <level>=[true|false]<br>
Switches this particular level on or off. (E.g. VERB_6=true)

For more information please refer to the examples of the framework.

### Loggers of KEO SPINE:
  * %keo_default_security_handler
  * %keo_ship_stack
  * %keo_ship_discovery
  * %keo_spine_transport
  * %keo_spine
  * %keo_util

### Loggers of the examples:
  * %example_client
  * %example_server
